**Definition 1.2 (unchanged common-law coordinates).** Use the source
dictionary FC.55–FC.58 and FC.81–FC.82. For $x=(u,v)\in S^1$ put
$h=(1,u/\sqrt\alpha,v/\sqrt\beta)$ and abbreviate
$c(x)=\mathfrak c(h)$, $\ell(x)=\ell(h)$ and $f(x)=f(h)$.
The source amplitude $c_{\rm src}$ is distinct from $c(x)$. In particular

$$
\begin{gathered}
\alpha+\beta=1,\qquad
f(x)=x^T\mathsf F x=2euv-d_*v^2,\qquad
\mathsf F=\begin{pmatrix}0&e\\e&-d_*\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\chi=2e/d_*,\\
\ell(x)=A_\ell u^2-2a_\ell uv+B_\ell v^2+l_u u+l_v v,\qquad
c(x)=N_c(x)/D_c(x)>0.
\end{gathered}
\tag{RB.1}
$$

For finite $t\ge t_0$ the cylinder, complete moments, contrast and
within-label absolute height are exactly FC.3 and GC.56:

$$
\begin{gathered}
X=S^1\times[0,1],\qquad b_x=tc(x)\xi,\\
\Gamma(x,\xi)=(1,u,v,u^2,uv,\xi c,\xi cu^2,\xi cuv)^T,\\
B(t)=(1,0,0,\alpha,0,\mu/t,\alpha V/t,0)^T,\\
\int_X\Gamma\,d\pi=B(t),\qquad
K(\pi)=\int_X|u|\,d\pi,\qquad
J_t(\pi)=\int_X|\ell+tc\xi f|\,d\pi,\\
m_u=\alpha V>0,\qquad m_v=\beta W>0,\qquad
\Lambda_f=\operatorname{diag}(m_u,m_v),\qquad m_u+m_v=\mu.
\end{gathered}
\tag{RB.2}
$$

A finite law has nonnegative coefficient masses summing to one; zero
masses are omitted. These masses are not physical acquisition or
failure probabilities. The source failure moments imply
$\int b_xxx^T\,d\pi=\Lambda_f$, including its zero mixed entry.
All first and mixed live moments in (RB.2) are retained.

